# verdict: satisfiable
# Source: ty mdtest bidirectional.md:1133
#   Bidirectional type inference / Generic call argument inference
#   (third code block; the call is at line 1249)
# ty: `two_lists(lst(X()), lst(X()))` has type X. The inner calls reveal
# list[X | int] and list[X | str].
#
#   def lst[T](x: T) -> list[T]
#   def two_lists[T](x: list[T | int], y: list[T | str]) -> T
#
# Each call gives its type parameter a fresh metavariable: ?A and ?B for the
# two `lst` calls, and ?T for `two_lists`. Each inner result is an argument
# of the outer call. list is invariant, so ?A must equal ?T | int, and ?B
# must equal ?T | str. Each inner argument puts X into its metavariable, so
# X must fit ?T | int and ?T | str. X fits neither int nor str, so X must go
# into ?T. The revealed result type X is an upper bound on ?T. The solver
# must split an equality between a metavariable and a union that contains
# another metavariable.
#
# The same unit has this call with list displays ([X()]), which need
# contextual display typing (out of scope). Its dict versions (`dct`,
# `two_dicts`) have the same structure with an `Any` value type, and its
# `*_default` versions differ only by a TypeVar default. They are not
# repeated.
class int;
class str;
class X;
class list[T];
metavar ?A;
metavar ?B;
metavar ?T;

assert lst_a: X <: ?A;
assert lst_b: X <: ?B;
assert two_lists_x: list[?A] <: list[?T | int];
assert two_lists_y: list[?B] <: list[?T | str];
assert result: ?T <: X;
